          An inverter generator does two things a conventional generator cannot: it produces electricity clean enough for anything with a circuit board, and it throttles its engine down when the load is light, which is why it can be quiet enough to sit beside at a campsite. Those benefits cost real money — roughly double per watt versus a conventional portable — so the question is not whether inverters are better, but whether you actually need what they do. Here is the honest answer, plus the seven units worth buying.
