        <!-- SECTION 6: Generator Safety -->
        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Generator Safety
          </h2>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Carbon Monoxide Kills Quietly and Fast</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Never run any fuel-burning generator indoors, in a garage, on a covered porch, or within 20 feet of a window, door, or vent — including inverter models, and including models with CO shutoff sensors. Generator exhaust is odorless and can reach fatal concentrations within minutes in an enclosed space. Install battery-powered carbon monoxide detectors inside the home as a second layer of protection.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Beyond carbon monoxide, three practices matter. Never refuel a hot or running engine — spilled gasoline on hot exhaust is a genuine fire risk and one of the most common causes of generator injuries. Keep the unit dry and elevated during wet weather using a proper generator canopy rather than a tarp, which traps exhaust heat. And never connect a generator to house wiring without a properly installed transfer switch: backfeeding through a dryer outlet energizes the utility line outside and can kill line workers repairing the outage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Maintenance That Prevents No-Starts</h3>
          <p>
            The overwhelming majority of generators that fail to start have a carburetor gummed by stale gasoline. Run the unit under load for 20-30 minutes every month or two, and either stabilize the fuel or shut the fuel valve and let the carburetor bowl run dry before storage. Change oil after the first 20-25 hours and every 50-100 hours after that — these are small sumps and oil degrades quickly.
          </p>
        </div>

