            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on Running Watts and Ignoring Starting Watts</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A refrigerator runs on 180 watts and needs 1,000 to start. A well pump runs on 1,200 and needs 4,000. Add running watts for everything on at once, then confirm the starting rating covers your largest motor's surge on top of that total, or the generator will trip at the worst moment.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying Inverter Prices for a Job Site</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Inverters cost roughly double per watt, and what you buy with that premium is quiet operation and clean power. On a job site running saws and compressors where nobody cares about noise, a conventional generator delivers far more capability for the money.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the Marketing Decibel Number</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Published noise figures are measured at quarter load or in eco mode. The same generator runs 6-10 dB louder under real household load — enough to change it from 'quiet' to 'noticeable'. Compare figures at comparable loads, and remember placement matters more than the last few decibels.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing It With Untreated Gasoline</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A gummed carburetor is the single most common reason a stored generator will not start when the power goes out. Either add stabilizer and run it through the carburetor, or shut the fuel valve and let the bowl run dry. Then exercise the unit under load every month or two.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Backfeeding Through a Dryer Outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Connecting a generator to house wiring without a transfer switch energizes the utility line outside your home and can kill line workers repairing the outage. It is also illegal in most jurisdictions. A properly installed transfer switch is a few hundred dollars and is not optional.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

